  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on the subjective perceptions of institutional stakeholders rather than direct biophysical measurements of ecosystem degradation.",
      "what": "This study applied a multi-criteria analysis to map and evaluate how institutional stakeholders perceive the impacts of tourism and urban growth on 12 distinct ecosystems in the Yucatan Peninsula, including coastal dunes, mangroves, cenotes, and tropical forests. The analysis reveals that stakeholders consistently prioritize regulatory contributions like water purification and storm protection in coastal ecosystems, while valuing cultivated grasslands primarily for food production. Perceived threats and ecosystem vulnerabilities varied by state, reflecting localized development trajectories.",
      "why": "It maps subjective stakeholder values and perceived ecological threats across specific administrative boundaries, revealing how regional development policies systematically trade long-term regulatory ecosystem services for short-term economic gains."
    }
  },
